    "sourceSha256": "edcbc737e4efa7c0e68d8fa8a91577d3fed6f566c4f04bd51ca6c0072ad904bf",
    "sourceWikitext": "{{recipesummary\n| category = Stew recipes\n| servings = 6\n| time = Cooking: 1 hour<br>Total: 1 day\n| difficulty = 3\n| Image = [[Image:Frijoles_Vanegas.jpg|300px]]\n}}{{recipe}} | [[Cookbook:Cuisine of Colombia|Colombia]] | [[Cookbook:Cereal Grain|Cereal Grain]] | [[Cookbook:Legumes|Legumes]]\n\n'''Antioqueño beans''' is an icon of [[Cookbook:Cuisine of Colombia|Colombian food]].\n\n==Ingredients==\n* 1 [[Cookbook:pound|lb]] [[Cookbook:Kidney Bean|red]] or [[Cookbook:Cargamanto|Cargamanto]] [[Cookbook:Bean|beans]]\n* 2 red [[Cookbook:Tomato|tomatoes]]\n* 1 [[Cookbook:Cup|cup]] [[Cookbook:Chopping|chopped]] [[Cookbook:Scallion|scallions]]\n* ¼ cup [[Cookbook:Oil|oil]]\n* 2 [[Cookbook:Tablespoon|tbsp]] [[Cookbook:Cumin|cumin]]\n* 1 tbsp [[Cookbook:Allspice|allspice]]\n* 6 cups [[Cookbook:Water|water]]\n* 1 chicken [[Cookbook:Dehydrated Broth|broth cube]]\n* 1 large [[Cookbook:Plantain|plantain]]\n* ½ [[Cookbook:pound|lb]] flank [[Cookbook:Beef|steak]], [[Cookbook:Pork|pork]] legs, or pork rinds (optional)\n\n==Procedure==\n# Wash beans and let hydrate overnight by covering with twice its volume of water.\n# The next day, remove any floating beans.\n# Heat the oil in a [[Cookbook:Pressure Cooker|pressure cooker]]. Add scallions and let [[Cookbook:Frying|fry]] until they start to brown. Add tomato, cumin, broth cube, and allspice. This is called \"hogao\", a basic sauce for many Colombian dishes.\n# Add 6 cups water, the beans, and plantain. Bring to a [[Cookbook:Boiling|boil]] over medium heat.\n# Cover the pressure cooker, and let cook for 30 minutes.\n# Let cool, then open the pressure cooker.\n# Check water level, add the meat if using, and cover again. Cook for another 30 minutes.\n# Taste for salt. If you want a thicker mixture, you can cook uncovered over low heat.\n\n==Notes, tips, and variations==\n* When serving, you can put additional hogao on top.\n* You can serve with white rice, avocado, ground beef, roasted beef, fried plantain, roasted chicken, corn arepas, and many other combinations.\n* Trasnochado beans, eaten one day after being cooked, are a great option to eat the beans with a delicious concentrated flavor.\n* To make bandeja paisa, serve a portion of Antioqueño beans, a portion of white rice, a portion of ground beef, a slice of avocado, one fried chorizo sausage, fried ripe plantain, one fried blood sausage, pork rind, and one fried egg on top of the rice.\n* Another delicious way to serve these beans is with cazuelitas: serve half of a cazuelita with the beans, and add to it corn, shredded beef (ropa vieja), avocado, and ground pork rind.\n\n[[Category:Colombian recipes]]\n[[Category:Recipes using pulses]]\n[[Category:Recipes for stew]]\n[[Category:Recipes for soup]]\n[[Category:Boiled recipes]]\n[[Category:Recipes using allspice]]\n[[Category:Recipes using dehydrated broth]]\n[[Category:Recipes using cargamanto bean]]\n[[Category:Recipes using cumin]]\n[[Category:Recipes using green onion]]",
    "isRecipe": true,
